          "content": "208"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.145,
            0.527,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "The number of possible outcomes = 52"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.187,
            0.258,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.175,
            0.508,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{F}) = \\frac{48}{52} = \\frac{12}{13}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.226,
            0.901,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "**Remark :** Note that F is nothing but E . Therefore, we can also calculate P(F) as"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.257,
            0.562,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "follows: $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{F})=\\mathrm{P}(\\overline{\\mathrm{E}})=1-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E})=1-\\frac{1}{13}=\\frac{12}{13}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.313,
            0.902,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5 :** Two players, Sangeeta and Reshma, play a tennis match. It is known that the probability of Sangeeta winning the match is 0.62. What is the probability of Reshma winning the match?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.379,
            0.901,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Let S and R denote the events that Sangeeta wins the match and Reshma wins the match, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.427,
            0.705,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "The probability of Sangeeta’s winnin $\\mathrm{g}=\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{S})=0.62\\ (\\mathrm{given})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.455,
            0.668,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "The probability of Reshma’s winning $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{R}) = 1 - \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{S})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.472,
            0.481,
            0.9,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "[As the events R and S are complementary]"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.477,
            0.505,
            0.661,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": "1 - 0.62 = 0.38",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.538,
            0.901,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6 :** Savita and Hamida are friends. What is the probability that both will have (i) different birthdays? (ii) the same birthday? (ignoring a leap year)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.583,
            0.9,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Out of the two friends, one girl, say, Savita’s birthday can be any day of the year. Now, Hamida’s birthday can also be any day of 365 days in the year."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.627,
            0.621,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": "We assume that these 365 outcomes are equally likely."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.652,
            0.901,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "(i) If Hamida’s birthday is different from Savita’s, the number of favourable outcomes for her birthday is $3 6 5 - 1 1 = 3 6 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.698,
            0.801,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "So, P (Hamida’s birthday is different from Savita’s birthday) $= \\frac{364}{365}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.742,
            0.595,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "(ii) P(Savita and Hamida have the same birthday)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.765,
            0.832,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}&= 1 -  P ( both have different brightness ) \\\\&= 1 - \\frac{364}{365} \\quad [ Using P (\\overline{ E }) = 1 -  P ( E )] \\\\&= \\frac{1}{365}\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
